1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,351
This is Australia.

2
00:00:01,351 --> 00:00:03,840
A country the size of a continent.

3
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:06,782
It's almost as big as the mainland United

4
00:00:06,782 --> 00:00:07,275
States.

5
00:00:07,275 --> 00:00:09,867
But while the United States has more than

6
00:00:09,867 --> 00:00:11,718
three hundred million people,

7
00:00:11,718 --> 00:00:14,680
Australia has only around twenty-seven million.

8
00:00:15,030 --> 00:00:17,331
And almost all of them live here,

9
00:00:17,331 --> 00:00:18,482
along the edges.

10
00:00:18,482 --> 00:00:21,122
Mostly in the east and the south-east,

11
00:00:21,122 --> 00:00:23,220
and in one corner of the west.

12
00:00:23,570 --> 00:00:24,413
The middle,

13
00:00:24,413 --> 00:00:27,573
an area bigger than most countries on Earth,

14
00:00:27,573 --> 00:00:29,540
is almost completely empty.

15
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:31,803
So why does almost no one live in

16
00:00:31,803 --> 00:00:33,210
the middle of Australia?

17
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,945
By the end of this video,

18
00:00:37,945 --> 00:00:39,868
you'll see how sinking air,

19
00:00:39,868 --> 00:00:41,309
a wall of mountains,

20
00:00:41,309 --> 00:00:44,193
ancient soil and a few brave and stubborn

21
00:00:44,193 --> 00:00:47,420
people drew the map of where Australians live.

22
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:51,475
Australia covers around seven point seven million square

23
00:00:51,475 --> 00:00:52,255
kilometers.

24
00:00:52,255 --> 00:00:55,180
It's the sixth largest country in the world,

25
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:57,715
and the only one that covers an entire

26
00:00:57,715 --> 00:00:58,430
continent.

27
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,641
To get a sense of the scale,

28
00:01:00,641 --> 00:01:01,924
put it over Europe.

29
00:01:01,924 --> 00:01:04,362
From Perth to Sydney is almost as far

30
00:01:04,362 --> 00:01:06,030
as from Madrid to Moscow.

31
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:07,325
Spread evenly,

32
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:10,097
Australia would have only about three and a

33
00:01:10,097 --> 00:01:12,240
half people per square kilometer.

34
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:14,886
That's one of the lowest densities of any

35
00:01:14,886 --> 00:01:16,020
country on Earth.

36
00:01:16,370 --> 00:01:18,559
Compare that with the United Kingdom,

37
00:01:18,559 --> 00:01:20,518
at around two hundred and eighty,

38
00:01:20,518 --> 00:01:21,785
or the United States,

39
00:01:21,785 --> 00:01:23,110
at around thirty-five.

40
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:25,915
But just like in other giant countries,

41
00:01:25,915 --> 00:01:27,572
the average is misleading.

42
00:01:27,572 --> 00:01:30,456
Because Australians are not spread out at all.

43
00:01:30,456 --> 00:01:31,990
They're packed together.

44
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:35,649
About two out of every three Australians live

45
00:01:35,649 --> 00:01:38,310
in just a handful of capital cities.

46
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:40,104
Sydney and Melbourne,

47
00:01:40,104 --> 00:01:42,597
with around five million people each.

48
00:01:42,597 --> 00:01:43,581
Then Brisbane,

49
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:44,893
Perth and Adelaide.

50
00:01:44,893 --> 00:01:47,190
Every one of them is on the coast.

51
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:50,420
And more than eight in ten Australians live

52
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:53,170
within about fifty kilometers of the sea.

53
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:54,777
Now flip it around.

54
00:01:54,777 --> 00:01:57,733
The areas officially classed as remote or very

55
00:01:57,733 --> 00:01:59,870
remote cover most of the country,

56
00:01:59,870 --> 00:02:02,134
but they are home to only about two

57
00:02:02,134 --> 00:02:03,580
percent of the people.

58
00:02:03,930 --> 00:02:06,279
So the question is not why Australia has

59
00:02:06,279 --> 00:02:06,967
few people.

60
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:09,087
It's why almost none of them live in

61
00:02:09,087 --> 00:02:10,920
the heart of their own country.

62
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:15,213
Let's go to the middle.

63
00:02:15,213 --> 00:02:17,564
Right near the center of the continent is

64
00:02:17,564 --> 00:02:19,075
the town of Alice Springs,

65
00:02:19,075 --> 00:02:21,370
with around twenty-five thousand people.

66
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:24,026
The nearest big city is Adelaide,

67
00:02:24,026 --> 00:02:27,620
around one thousand three hundred kilometers away in

68
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:28,773
a straight line.

69
00:02:28,773 --> 00:02:31,214
Darwin is about as far in the other

70
00:02:31,214 --> 00:02:31,960
direction.

71
00:02:32,310 --> 00:02:35,323
Around four hundred and fifty kilometers away by

72
00:02:35,323 --> 00:02:36,246
road is Uluru,

73
00:02:36,246 --> 00:02:39,197
the giant sandstone rock that rises about three

74
00:02:39,197 --> 00:02:41,780
hundred and fifty meters above the plain.

75
00:02:42,130 --> 00:02:44,139
It is sacred to the Anangu,

76
00:02:44,139 --> 00:02:47,511
the Aboriginal traditional owners of the land,

77
00:02:47,511 --> 00:02:48,659
and since 2019,

78
00:02:48,659 --> 00:02:50,740
climbing it has been banned.

79
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:53,626
All around it is the outback.

80
00:02:53,626 --> 00:02:54,302
A huge,

81
00:02:54,302 --> 00:02:56,839
dry interior made of deserts,

82
00:02:56,839 --> 00:02:58,022
rocky ranges,

83
00:02:58,022 --> 00:03:00,220
scrubland and salt lakes.

84
00:03:00,570 --> 00:03:02,409
The largest of these deserts,

85
00:03:02,409 --> 00:03:04,065
the Great Victoria Desert,

86
00:03:04,065 --> 00:03:07,376
covers around three hundred and fifty thousand square

87
00:03:07,376 --> 00:03:08,786
kilometers on its own.

88
00:03:08,786 --> 00:03:10,380
That's bigger than Italy.

89
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:12,184
The Northern Territory,

90
00:03:12,184 --> 00:03:14,305
which includes much of the center,

91
00:03:14,305 --> 00:03:16,668
is more than twice the size of France.

92
00:03:16,668 --> 00:03:18,849
But it has only around a quarter of

93
00:03:18,849 --> 00:03:19,940
a million people.

94
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:22,594
And in the south lies Lake Eyre,

95
00:03:22,594 --> 00:03:23,711
or Kati Thanda.

96
00:03:23,711 --> 00:03:26,574
At about fifteen meters below sea level,

97
00:03:26,574 --> 00:03:29,087
it's the lowest point in Australia,

98
00:03:29,087 --> 00:03:31,810
and the largest lake on the continent.

99
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:33,793
Except most of the time,

100
00:03:33,793 --> 00:03:35,360
it isn't a lake at all.

101
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:37,058
It's a crust of dry salt.

102
00:03:37,058 --> 00:03:39,867
It has filled completely only a handful of

103
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:41,630
times since records began.

104
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:43,312
When it does fill,

105
00:03:43,312 --> 00:03:45,696
after big rains far to the north,

106
00:03:45,696 --> 00:03:47,379
the desert comes alive.

107
00:03:47,379 --> 00:03:50,814
Huge flocks of pelicans and other waterbirds fly

108
00:03:50,814 --> 00:03:52,147
in from the coast,

109
00:03:52,147 --> 00:03:54,180
hundreds of kilometers away.

110
00:03:54,530 --> 00:03:56,304
And the heat is extreme.

111
00:03:56,304 --> 00:04:00,277
Australia's official record is around fifty point seven

112
00:04:00,277 --> 00:04:01,483
degrees Celsius,

113
00:04:01,483 --> 00:04:04,746
measured in the outback town of Oodnadatta in

114
00:04:04,746 --> 00:04:05,172
1960,

115
00:04:05,172 --> 00:04:08,010
and equalled on the west coast in 2022.

116
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:09,320
In the 1920s,

117
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:12,267
the mining town of Marble Bar recorded one

118
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:14,872
hundred and sixty days in a row above

119
00:04:14,872 --> 00:04:17,202
thirty-seven point eight degrees.

120
00:04:17,202 --> 00:04:19,190
That's still a world record.

121
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:20,037
So yes,

122
00:04:20,037 --> 00:04:21,280
the outback is hot.

123
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:23,890
But heat alone doesn't empty a continent.

124
00:04:23,890 --> 00:04:26,003
Plenty of hot places are crowded.

125
00:04:26,003 --> 00:04:28,240
The real problem is something else.

126
00:04:31,190 --> 00:04:33,198
The real problem is water.

127
00:04:33,198 --> 00:04:34,908
Apart from Antarctica,

128
00:04:34,908 --> 00:04:38,180
Australia is the driest continent on Earth.

129
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:42,410
Around seventy percent of the mainland is classed

130
00:04:42,410 --> 00:04:44,117
as arid or semi-arid.

131
00:04:44,117 --> 00:04:46,367
That means very little rain,

132
00:04:46,367 --> 00:04:47,841
and in many years,

133
00:04:47,841 --> 00:04:48,850
almost none.

134
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:51,846
The first reason is where Australia sits.

135
00:04:51,846 --> 00:04:53,799
Look at this band of latitude,

136
00:04:53,799 --> 00:04:55,627
around thirty degrees south.

137
00:04:55,627 --> 00:04:58,147
It runs right through the middle of the

138
00:04:58,147 --> 00:04:58,840
continent.

139
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:00,501
Near the equator,

140
00:05:00,501 --> 00:05:03,050
warm air rises and drops its rain.

141
00:05:03,050 --> 00:05:06,182
Then that air flows away from the equator,

142
00:05:06,182 --> 00:05:06,692
cools,

143
00:05:06,692 --> 00:05:10,187
and sinks again around thirty degrees north and

144
00:05:10,187 --> 00:05:10,697
south.

145
00:05:10,697 --> 00:05:12,154
Sinking air is dry,

146
00:05:12,154 --> 00:05:14,630
and it stops clouds from forming.

147
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:17,377
That's the same reason the Sahara,

148
00:05:17,377 --> 00:05:20,322
the Arabian Desert and the Kalahari exist.

149
00:05:20,322 --> 00:05:23,198
Australia simply has a huge piece of land

150
00:05:23,198 --> 00:05:25,390
sitting right in that dry belt.

151
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:27,748
The second reason is a wall.

152
00:05:27,748 --> 00:05:30,864
Along the east coast runs the Great Dividing

153
00:05:30,864 --> 00:05:31,349
Range,

154
00:05:31,349 --> 00:05:34,950
stretching for more than three thousand kilometers.

155
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:38,484
Moist winds from the Pacific hit those mountains

156
00:05:38,484 --> 00:05:39,133
and rise.

157
00:05:39,133 --> 00:05:40,303
As the air cools,

158
00:05:40,303 --> 00:05:42,837
it drops its rain on the coastal side.

159
00:05:42,837 --> 00:05:45,241
By the time it crosses to the inland

160
00:05:45,241 --> 00:05:45,631
side,

161
00:05:45,631 --> 00:05:47,580
much of the moisture is gone.

162
00:05:47,930 --> 00:05:49,244
That's why the green,

163
00:05:49,244 --> 00:05:51,035
fertile strip is in the east.

164
00:05:51,035 --> 00:05:53,185
It's the wet side of the mountains.

165
00:05:53,185 --> 00:05:56,290
And it's exactly where most Australians live today.

166
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:59,232
The third reason is the shape of the

167
00:05:59,232 --> 00:06:00,143
land itself.

168
00:06:00,143 --> 00:06:02,735
Australia is the flattest continent.

169
00:06:02,735 --> 00:06:04,276
Its highest mountain,

170
00:06:04,276 --> 00:06:05,537
Mount Kosciuszko,

171
00:06:05,537 --> 00:06:08,760
is only about two thousand two hundred meters

172
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:09,180
high.

173
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:12,155
Without high mountains in the interior,

174
00:06:12,155 --> 00:06:14,714
there is little to push air upward and

175
00:06:14,714 --> 00:06:16,288
squeeze rain out of it.

176
00:06:16,288 --> 00:06:18,060
The clouds just pass over.

177
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:20,498
Australia's biggest river system,

178
00:06:20,498 --> 00:06:22,217
the Murray and the Darling,

179
00:06:22,217 --> 00:06:24,796
drains around a seventh of the continent.

180
00:06:24,796 --> 00:06:27,252
It's one of the main farming regions of

181
00:06:27,252 --> 00:06:28,050
the country.

182
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:30,658
But even this river can slow to a

183
00:06:30,658 --> 00:06:31,986
trickle in drought,

184
00:06:31,986 --> 00:06:34,377
and in some dry years its mouth has

185
00:06:34,377 --> 00:06:37,100
needed dredging to stay open to the sea.

186
00:06:37,450 --> 00:06:38,670
Under the outback,

187
00:06:38,670 --> 00:06:39,954
there's a surprise.

188
00:06:39,954 --> 00:06:41,624
The Great Artesian Basin,

189
00:06:41,624 --> 00:06:44,771
one of the largest underground water reserves in

190
00:06:44,771 --> 00:06:45,477
the world,

191
00:06:45,477 --> 00:06:48,110
lies beneath about a fifth of Australia.

192
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:51,364
It keeps cattle and some towns alive.

193
00:06:51,364 --> 00:06:54,039
But much of that water is ancient,

194
00:06:54,039 --> 00:06:55,339
slowly refilled,

195
00:06:55,339 --> 00:06:58,014
and often too salty or too deep to

196
00:06:58,014 --> 00:07:00,230
support big farms or cities.

197
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:02,434
And then there's the soil.

198
00:07:02,434 --> 00:07:05,523
Australia is one of the oldest landmasses on

199
00:07:05,523 --> 00:07:06,003
Earth.

200
00:07:06,003 --> 00:07:09,161
Its rocks have been weathered for hundreds of

201
00:07:09,161 --> 00:07:10,466
millions of years,

202
00:07:10,466 --> 00:07:13,623
with few volcanoes or glaciers to bring fresh

203
00:07:13,623 --> 00:07:14,310
minerals.

204
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:17,909
So the middle of Australia has three problems

205
00:07:17,909 --> 00:07:18,544
at once.

206
00:07:18,544 --> 00:07:19,745
Sinking dry air,

207
00:07:19,745 --> 00:07:21,934
mountains that steal the rain,

208
00:07:21,934 --> 00:07:24,900
and old soils that are poor in nutrients.

209
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:28,415
And yet,

210
00:07:28,415 --> 00:07:30,925
people have lived in the outback for an

211
00:07:30,925 --> 00:07:32,620
extraordinarily long time.

212
00:07:32,970 --> 00:07:36,678
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived

213
00:07:36,678 --> 00:07:39,800
in Australia for at least fifty thousand years,

214
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,662
and possibly more than sixty-five thousand.

215
00:07:42,662 --> 00:07:45,655
It's one of the oldest continuous cultures on

216
00:07:45,655 --> 00:07:46,110
Earth.

217
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:49,535
Desert peoples survived in the harshest parts of

218
00:07:49,535 --> 00:07:53,049
the continent with knowledge passed down over countless

219
00:07:53,049 --> 00:07:53,865
generations:

220
00:07:53,865 --> 00:07:55,183
where to find water,

221
00:07:55,183 --> 00:07:56,061
when to move,

222
00:07:56,061 --> 00:07:57,630
and what could be eaten.

223
00:07:57,980 --> 00:07:58,822
Rock holes,

224
00:07:58,822 --> 00:08:02,543
soaks and springs were remembered and shared through

225
00:08:02,543 --> 00:08:03,175
stories,

226
00:08:03,175 --> 00:08:04,228
songs and law,

227
00:08:04,228 --> 00:08:08,300
sometimes connecting places hundreds of kilometers apart.

228
00:08:08,650 --> 00:08:10,128
But notice something.

229
00:08:10,128 --> 00:08:11,875
Desert groups were small,

230
00:08:11,875 --> 00:08:14,563
and they moved across very large areas.

231
00:08:14,563 --> 00:08:16,646
The land could support people,

232
00:08:16,646 --> 00:08:18,460
but not many in one place.

233
00:08:18,810 --> 00:08:22,147
Aboriginal people also shaped the land itself,

234
00:08:22,147 --> 00:08:25,909
using carefully controlled fires to renew plants and

235
00:08:25,909 --> 00:08:27,116
attract animals.

236
00:08:27,116 --> 00:08:27,613
Today,

237
00:08:27,613 --> 00:08:30,808
this cultural burning is again being used to

238
00:08:30,808 --> 00:08:32,370
help manage the bush.

239
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:34,722
Even before Europeans arrived,

240
00:08:34,722 --> 00:08:37,821
the population was much denser along the wetter

241
00:08:37,821 --> 00:08:39,048
coasts and rivers,

242
00:08:39,048 --> 00:08:40,146
like the Murray,

243
00:08:40,146 --> 00:08:41,760
than in the deep desert.

244
00:08:42,110 --> 00:08:44,943
So the pattern we see today is ancient.

245
00:08:44,943 --> 00:08:48,129
Water has always decided how many people the

246
00:08:48,129 --> 00:08:49,333
land could hold.

247
00:08:49,333 --> 00:08:50,679
And in the middle,

248
00:08:50,679 --> 00:08:52,662
the answer has always been:

249
00:08:52,662 --> 00:08:53,370
very few.

250
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:56,946
In 1788,

251
00:08:56,946 --> 00:09:00,286
the First Fleet of eleven British ships arrived

252
00:09:00,286 --> 00:09:01,400
at Sydney Cove,

253
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:04,879
carrying around seven hundred and fifty convicts,

254
00:09:04,879 --> 00:09:05,923
plus soldiers,

255
00:09:05,923 --> 00:09:08,010
officials and their families.

256
00:09:08,360 --> 00:09:09,971
Over the next decades,

257
00:09:09,971 --> 00:09:11,933
more colonies were founded.

258
00:09:11,933 --> 00:09:14,035
Each one had its own capital,

259
00:09:14,035 --> 00:09:16,137
and every capital was a port.

260
00:09:16,137 --> 00:09:16,698
Hobart,

261
00:09:16,698 --> 00:09:17,398
Brisbane,

262
00:09:17,398 --> 00:09:18,169
Melbourne,

263
00:09:18,169 --> 00:09:18,659
Perth,

264
00:09:18,659 --> 00:09:19,360
Adelaide.

265
00:09:19,710 --> 00:09:21,002
That made sense.

266
00:09:21,002 --> 00:09:23,509
Everything came and went by sea:

267
00:09:23,509 --> 00:09:24,117
people,

268
00:09:24,117 --> 00:09:24,877
supplies,

269
00:09:24,877 --> 00:09:26,017
wool and gold.

270
00:09:26,017 --> 00:09:29,512
Each colony was more connected to London than

271
00:09:29,512 --> 00:09:30,880
to its neighbors.

272
00:09:31,230 --> 00:09:31,835
In fact,

273
00:09:31,835 --> 00:09:35,463
the colonies even built their railways with different

274
00:09:35,463 --> 00:09:36,404
track widths.

275
00:09:36,404 --> 00:09:39,427
Trains from one colony often couldn't run on

276
00:09:39,427 --> 00:09:41,040
the tracks of the next.

277
00:09:41,390 --> 00:09:44,050
It wasn't until 1970 that a train could

278
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:46,510
run all the way from Sydney to Perth

279
00:09:46,510 --> 00:09:48,040
on one standard track.

280
00:09:48,390 --> 00:09:49,180
Meanwhile,

281
00:09:49,180 --> 00:09:52,773
the interior remained a mystery to the colonists.

282
00:09:52,773 --> 00:09:55,790
Some even believed there might be a great

283
00:09:55,790 --> 00:09:58,880
inland sea in the middle of the continent.

284
00:09:59,230 --> 00:09:59,854
In 1860,

285
00:09:59,854 --> 00:10:02,907
the Burke and Wills expedition set out from

286
00:10:02,907 --> 00:10:06,168
Melbourne to cross the continent from south to

287
00:10:06,168 --> 00:10:06,653
north.

288
00:10:06,653 --> 00:10:09,845
They reached the tidal channels near the Gulf

289
00:10:09,845 --> 00:10:10,955
of Carpentaria,

290
00:10:10,955 --> 00:10:12,412
but on the way back,

291
00:10:12,412 --> 00:10:13,730
both leaders died.

292
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:14,987
A year later,

293
00:10:14,987 --> 00:10:18,227
John McDouall Stuart crossed from Adelaide to the

294
00:10:18,227 --> 00:10:20,301
north coast and returned alive.

295
00:10:20,301 --> 00:10:22,634
His route became the path of one of

296
00:10:22,634 --> 00:10:25,680
the great engineering projects of the century.

297
00:10:26,030 --> 00:10:26,676
In 1872,

298
00:10:26,676 --> 00:10:29,689
the Overland Telegraph Line was finished.

299
00:10:29,689 --> 00:10:33,420
About three thousand two hundred kilometers of wire

300
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:35,213
from Adelaide to Darwin,

301
00:10:35,213 --> 00:10:38,227
where it joined an undersea cable to Asia

302
00:10:38,227 --> 00:10:39,590
and on to Britain.

303
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:42,706
News from London that once took weeks by

304
00:10:42,706 --> 00:10:44,528
ship now arrived in hours.

305
00:10:44,528 --> 00:10:45,877
And along the line,

306
00:10:45,877 --> 00:10:49,183
small repeater stations were built in the desert

307
00:10:49,183 --> 00:10:50,600
to boost the signal.

308
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:53,255
One of those stations was built by a

309
00:10:53,255 --> 00:10:55,872
waterhole in the center of the continent.

310
00:10:55,872 --> 00:10:58,239
The town that grew around it is Alice

311
00:10:58,239 --> 00:10:58,800
Springs.

312
00:10:59,150 --> 00:11:01,596
So the most famous town in the outback

313
00:11:01,596 --> 00:11:04,043
didn't grow because the land was rich.

314
00:11:04,043 --> 00:11:06,489
It grew because a wire needed to cross

315
00:11:06,489 --> 00:11:06,740
it.

316
00:11:07,090 --> 00:11:08,616
If you're enjoying this,

317
00:11:08,616 --> 00:11:09,287
subscribe.

318
00:11:09,287 --> 00:11:11,667
Every week we find the story hidden in

319
00:11:11,667 --> 00:11:12,460
another map.

320
00:11:15,410 --> 00:11:16,167
Of course,

321
00:11:16,167 --> 00:11:18,986
settlers did try to farm further inland.

322
00:11:18,986 --> 00:11:21,531
And one man tried to warn them where

323
00:11:21,531 --> 00:11:22,150
to stop.

324
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:23,163
In 1865,

325
00:11:23,163 --> 00:11:24,932
after a severe drought,

326
00:11:24,932 --> 00:11:26,037
George Goyder,

327
00:11:26,037 --> 00:11:29,058
the surveyor general of South Australia,

328
00:11:29,058 --> 00:11:31,932
traveled the colony and drew a line on

329
00:11:31,932 --> 00:11:32,595
the map.

330
00:11:32,595 --> 00:11:33,553
North of it,

331
00:11:33,553 --> 00:11:34,217
he said,

332
00:11:34,217 --> 00:11:37,680
rainfall was too unreliable for growing crops.

333
00:11:38,030 --> 00:11:40,864
Then came a run of unusually wet years

334
00:11:40,864 --> 00:11:41,882
in the 1870s.

335
00:11:41,882 --> 00:11:45,007
Many people decided Goyder had been wrong,

336
00:11:45,007 --> 00:11:47,914
and farms and towns spread north of his

337
00:11:47,914 --> 00:11:48,350
line.

338
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:50,489
When the dry years returned,

339
00:11:50,489 --> 00:11:51,600
the crops failed.

340
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:54,808
Today you can still find abandoned stone farmhouses

341
00:11:54,808 --> 00:11:55,857
beyond the line,

342
00:11:55,857 --> 00:11:57,831
and it is still used as a guide

343
00:11:57,831 --> 00:11:59,620
for where cropping is risky.

344
00:11:59,970 --> 00:12:02,105
To move goods across the desert,

345
00:12:02,105 --> 00:12:04,240
the colonists brought in camels,

346
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:07,215
along with cameleers mostly from what are now

347
00:12:07,215 --> 00:12:08,056
Afghanistan,

348
00:12:08,056 --> 00:12:09,350
Pakistan and India.

349
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:12,301
When trucks and railways replaced them,

350
00:12:12,301 --> 00:12:14,057
many camels were released.

351
00:12:14,057 --> 00:12:17,308
Today Australia has hundreds of thousands of wild

352
00:12:17,308 --> 00:12:17,828
camels,

353
00:12:17,828 --> 00:12:20,755
one of the largest wild camel populations in

354
00:12:20,755 --> 00:12:21,470
the world.

355
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:24,700
The cameleers are remembered in the name of

356
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:25,748
a famous train,

357
00:12:25,748 --> 00:12:26,403
the Ghan,

358
00:12:26,403 --> 00:12:29,872
which today runs from Adelaide through Alice Springs

359
00:12:29,872 --> 00:12:30,592
to Darwin.

360
00:12:30,592 --> 00:12:33,080
The line only reached Darwin in 2004.

361
00:12:33,430 --> 00:12:34,946
Where crops failed,

362
00:12:34,946 --> 00:12:36,082
cattle worked.

363
00:12:36,082 --> 00:12:37,370
But on dry land,

364
00:12:37,370 --> 00:12:40,553
each animal needs a huge area of grazing.

365
00:12:40,553 --> 00:12:44,114
So the outback is divided into cattle stations

366
00:12:44,114 --> 00:12:45,630
of incredible size.

367
00:12:45,980 --> 00:12:46,809
The largest,

368
00:12:46,809 --> 00:12:49,294
Anna Creek Station in South Australia,

369
00:12:49,294 --> 00:12:52,736
covers around twenty-four thousand square kilometers.

370
00:12:52,736 --> 00:12:55,285
That's bigger than the whole of Israel,

371
00:12:55,285 --> 00:12:57,580
run by just a small team of people.

372
00:12:57,930 --> 00:12:59,468
Then there's mining.

373
00:12:59,468 --> 00:13:00,493
In the 1910s,

374
00:13:00,493 --> 00:13:02,763
opal was found at Coober Pedy.

375
00:13:02,763 --> 00:13:03,276
Today,

376
00:13:03,276 --> 00:13:07,230
Australia produces most of the world's precious opal.

377
00:13:07,580 --> 00:13:10,240
Summer days in Coober Pedy are so hot

378
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:12,900
that many residents live underground,

379
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:16,190
in homes dug into the hillside called dugouts,

380
00:13:16,190 --> 00:13:19,690
where the temperature stays comfortable all year.

381
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:22,833
Every one of these places shows the same

382
00:13:22,833 --> 00:13:23,310
thing.

383
00:13:23,310 --> 00:13:25,490
People can live in the outback.

384
00:13:25,490 --> 00:13:27,671
But only with a special reason,

385
00:13:27,671 --> 00:13:29,510
and only in small numbers.

386
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:34,807
So what's it like to live there now?

387
00:13:34,807 --> 00:13:37,026
The biggest challenge is distance.

388
00:13:37,026 --> 00:13:38,422
The nearest hospital,

389
00:13:38,422 --> 00:13:41,719
school or supermarket can be hundreds of kilometers

390
00:13:41,719 --> 00:13:42,100
away.

391
00:13:42,450 --> 00:13:43,141
In 1928,

392
00:13:43,141 --> 00:13:46,748
a minister called John Flynn started an aerial

393
00:13:46,748 --> 00:13:48,052
medical service.

394
00:13:48,052 --> 00:13:48,590
Today,

395
00:13:48,590 --> 00:13:52,427
the Royal Flying Doctor Service flies doctors and

396
00:13:52,427 --> 00:13:55,420
nurses to patients across the outback.

397
00:13:55,770 --> 00:13:56,754
And from 1951,

398
00:13:56,754 --> 00:14:00,164
children on remote stations could attend the School

399
00:14:00,164 --> 00:14:00,951
of the Air,

400
00:14:00,951 --> 00:14:03,508
first with lessons over two-way radio,

401
00:14:03,508 --> 00:14:05,410
and today over the internet.

402
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:08,945
The outback is also where much of Australia's

403
00:14:08,945 --> 00:14:10,261
wealth comes from.

404
00:14:10,261 --> 00:14:12,754
Huge iron ore mines in the Pilbara,

405
00:14:12,754 --> 00:14:14,070
in the north-west,

406
00:14:14,070 --> 00:14:17,671
have helped make Australia the world's largest iron

407
00:14:17,671 --> 00:14:18,640
ore exporter.

408
00:14:18,990 --> 00:14:21,094
But many miners don't live there.

409
00:14:21,094 --> 00:14:23,323
They fly in from Perth for a couple

410
00:14:23,323 --> 00:14:24,870
of weeks of long shifts,

411
00:14:24,870 --> 00:14:26,170
then fly home again.

412
00:14:26,170 --> 00:14:27,160
The mines grow,

413
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:28,460
but the towns don't.

414
00:14:28,810 --> 00:14:30,996
The far north is different again.

415
00:14:30,996 --> 00:14:31,961
It's tropical,

416
00:14:31,961 --> 00:14:32,733
not desert.

417
00:14:32,733 --> 00:14:35,048
But almost all of its rain falls in

418
00:14:35,048 --> 00:14:37,106
a few months of the wet season,

419
00:14:37,106 --> 00:14:38,328
cutting off roads,

420
00:14:38,328 --> 00:14:41,028
followed by months with almost no rain at

421
00:14:41,028 --> 00:14:41,350
all.

422
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:44,357
Even the largest inland city in Australia is

423
00:14:44,357 --> 00:14:44,947
Canberra,

424
00:14:44,947 --> 00:14:46,246
the national capital,

425
00:14:46,246 --> 00:14:49,434
with around four hundred and seventy thousand people.

426
00:14:49,434 --> 00:14:51,914
And it's only about one hundred and fifty

427
00:14:51,914 --> 00:14:53,390
kilometers from the sea.

428
00:14:53,740 --> 00:14:55,551
Canberra was a compromise.

429
00:14:55,551 --> 00:14:56,892
In the early 1900s,

430
00:14:56,892 --> 00:15:00,380
Sydney and Melbourne couldn't agree which should be

431
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:01,251
the capital,

432
00:15:01,251 --> 00:15:03,800
so a new city was built between them.

433
00:15:04,150 --> 00:15:05,144
Looking ahead,

434
00:15:05,144 --> 00:15:08,392
the outback's sunshine is among the strongest on

435
00:15:08,392 --> 00:15:08,856
Earth,

436
00:15:08,856 --> 00:15:11,110
and there are plans to use it for

437
00:15:11,110 --> 00:15:12,303
huge solar farms.

438
00:15:12,303 --> 00:15:13,496
But solar panels,

439
00:15:13,496 --> 00:15:14,292
like mines,

440
00:15:14,292 --> 00:15:15,750
need very few people.

441
00:15:16,100 --> 00:15:19,027
And climate change may make the dry interior

442
00:15:19,027 --> 00:15:19,873
even harder,

443
00:15:19,873 --> 00:15:22,670
with more intense heat waves and droughts.

444
00:15:23,020 --> 00:15:25,230
So let's put the whole story together.

445
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:28,347
The middle of Australia sits in the same

446
00:15:28,347 --> 00:15:29,967
dry belt as the Sahara.

447
00:15:29,967 --> 00:15:32,936
Mountains in the east catch the rain before

448
00:15:32,936 --> 00:15:34,420
it can travel inland.

449
00:15:34,420 --> 00:15:36,580
And the ancient soils are poor.

450
00:15:36,930 --> 00:15:40,344
The colonies grew as ports facing the sea,

451
00:15:40,344 --> 00:15:43,917
and every attempt to settle the interior ran

452
00:15:43,917 --> 00:15:45,584
into the same limit:

453
00:15:45,584 --> 00:15:46,140
water.

454
00:15:46,490 --> 00:15:48,708
That's why almost no one lives in the

455
00:15:48,708 --> 00:15:49,933
middle of Australia.

456
00:15:49,933 --> 00:15:51,451
Not because no one tried,

457
00:15:51,451 --> 00:15:53,902
but because the land and the climate have

458
00:15:53,902 --> 00:15:56,470
always decided how many people it can hold.

459
00:15:56,820 --> 00:16:01,106
Every map hides a story like this one.

460
00:16:01,106 --> 00:16:05,283
If you want to discover the next one,

461
00:16:05,283 --> 00:16:08,470
subscribe to Timeline Earth.
